00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:45.140
So that came with its own
set of fun challenges.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.090
We have our newly launched
lifestyle vertical,

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:48.042
which is called As/Is.

00:01:48.042 --> 00:01:50.000
And then we also have a
bunch of video content,

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.833
which lives on bunch
of different platforms.

00:01:51.833 --> 00:01:53.659
Of course, Tasty,
quite big on Facebook.

00:01:53.659 --> 00:01:56.200
A lot of people actually don't
realize that this is BuzzFeed,

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:57.650
but it is.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:01.192
And then also things like Worth
It, which live on YouTube.

00:02:01.192 --> 00:02:03.400
So that's actually kind of
a great segue into the way

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.870
that we think about our data,
and some of the problems

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:05.690
that we have to solve.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:07.540
So I'm just going to give
you guys some quick stats so

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:09.664
you can understand sort of
the scale of the problem

00:02:09.664 --> 00:02:10.850
that we're trying to solve.

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:13.137
So we get about 9 billion
monthly content views.

00:02:13.137 --> 00:02:15.220
That's coming from all of
our different platforms.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:17.386
That's BuzzFeed.com, that's
our apps, it's Facebook,

00:02:17.386 --> 00:02:21.010
it's YouTube, it's
Instagram, it's Snapchat.

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:24.040
About 200 million monthly
unique visitors to BuzzFeed.com

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:25.660
specifically.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:27.130
Tens of thousands of assets.

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:29.440
So we actually have
a unique problem,

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:31.930
taking all the statistics and
attaching them to metadata,

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:35.220
specifically for content that's
living on multiple platforms.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:38.950
And data from at
least 20 platforms.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:40.920
Now, because we're
a content company,

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.900
content analytics are kind of at
the center of everything we do.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:45.419
And we actually
have really placed

00:02:45.419 --> 00:02:47.710
a huge emphasis on making
the role not just of our data

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:49.600
science and analytics
team, but actually

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:51.730
everybody in the company,
and especially the people

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:52.930
who are making stuff.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:56.320
So this is actually a slide that
I stole from our CEO, Jonah,

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:58.384
who has a really great
philosophy for the way

00:02:58.384 --> 00:02:59.800
that he thinks
about these things.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:01.720
We like to think about these
things in terms of the feedback

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:02.803
loops that we're creating.

00:03:02.803 --> 00:03:05.440
So people who are making stuff
are at the center of everything

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.898
that we're doing, but they're
also pushing this content out

00:03:07.898 --> 00:03:09.250
to all different places--

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:11.890
to our site, to our apps, to all
of these different distributed

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:12.530
platforms.

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:14.780
And what we get back in
return is data and learnings,

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:17.404
and also, if we're lucky,
sometimes some money.

00:03:17.404 --> 00:03:19.320
But what that also means
is that our reality--

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:20.820
is that we're left
sort of grappling

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:23.491
with data coming in from all
of these different platforms.

00:03:23.491 --> 00:03:24.990
And this is actually
just a sampling

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:26.990
of the different things
that we're working with.

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:29.615
But as you can imagine, we sort
of are stuck with this problem.

00:03:29.615 --> 00:03:31.590
Things coming in different
formats, some stuff

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:33.390
is time series, some
stuff is aggregate.

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:36.220
So that's sort of the heart
of what we're trying to solve.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:38.700
So, great segue to our problem.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.770
As a data team, how
do we consolidate

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:43.660
all of this data in one place?

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:46.210
Make it easy for hundreds
of users with varying skill

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:47.560
sets to understand.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.600
So of course we have data
scientists, analysts,

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:51.750
engineers looking at the stuff.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:53.170
We also have content creators.

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:55.090
We also have people
in our business team.

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:56.650
So we have a really
broad spectrum

00:03:56.650 --> 00:04:00.220
in terms of people's expertise
and comfort with data, which

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:02.600
actually creates a sort of
unique problem of its own

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:05.266
in terms of the way that we want
to display this stuff to users,

00:04:05.266 --> 00:04:07.900
and how easy we need to make
it for them to understand.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:10.149
And then, of course, how do
accomplish these first two

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:12.460
goals without putting an
enormous and continual burden

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:13.877
on our data and
engineering teams.

00:04:13.877 --> 00:04:16.418
So that's actually going to be
a really central piece of what

00:04:16.418 --> 00:04:17.600
I talk about today.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.042
So how do we actually do it?

00:04:19.042 --> 00:04:21.250
I'm going to say some things
today that will probably

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:22.280
make it easier said than done.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:24.280
And I really want to emphasize
before I say any of those

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:25.738
that this was a
big process for us,

00:04:25.738 --> 00:04:27.910
and it really took
a village in terms

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:30.540
of the number of people,
the number of teams,

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:31.290
that worked on it.

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:33.250
And I actually have a
number of my great teammates

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:34.749
sitting here in the
fourth row here.

00:04:34.749 --> 00:04:36.930
So you can come
talk to us after.

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:40.880
So the first piece of this was
building a strong foundation.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.450
And again, I'm going to
oversimplify a little bit here.

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:46.860
But what it really came down to
for us was one critical piece,

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:49.490
and that was really just getting
everything in the same place.

00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:52.210
So for us, that was BigQuery.

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:55.960
Obviously, that's me presenting
a grossly oversimplified

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:57.525
version of what it
took to get there.

00:04:57.525 --> 00:04:59.650
So I can talk a little bit
about the infrastructure

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:00.657
that we used to do that.

00:05:00.657 --> 00:05:02.990
And if you guys have more
specific questions about this,

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:05.140
we can cover it more in Q&amp;A,
or you can come find us after,

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:06.973
and I'd be happy to
sort of chat through it.

00:05:06.973 --> 00:05:08.740
But basically,
what we ultimately

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:11.050
landed on is that in
order for this to work,

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:13.450
in order for us to
accomplish our goals,

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:16.060
we needed to funnel everything
through the same pipeline,

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:17.720
and land it in the same place.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:19.330
Now, we're very
fortunate at BuzzFeed

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:21.040
that one of our
VPs of engineering

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:23.020
was one of the people
who coauthored NSQ.

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:26.320
So we had a great data
pipeline to sit on top of.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:29.387
So everything runs through
there, and it lands in GCS,

00:05:29.387 --> 00:05:30.970
and then ultimately
in BigQuery, which

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:32.636
is sort of what we
can use to surface it

00:05:32.636 --> 00:05:34.000
back up to all of our users.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.880
You'll see some internal tools
that we built in here as well.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:38.620
I'll talk a little bit about
those but not too much.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:39.640
And again, if you
have questions,

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:40.640
just come find us after.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.540
We'd be happy to chat about it.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:44.290
So once you have that
foundation in place,

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:45.748
you have all your
data in BigQuery,

00:05:45.748 --> 00:05:49.420
now you have the real task ahead
of you of building the home

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:51.070
that your users are
going to live in.

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:52.550
And I like to think about
this in terms of building

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:54.040
with bricks and not
straw, because you're

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:55.623
trying to build
something that's going

00:05:55.623 --> 00:05:56.892
to last for the long haul.

00:05:56.892 --> 00:05:58.600
So when we thought
about this, we sort of

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:01.630
knew that we had two big groups
that we were building for.

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:03.340
One was going to
be the data team,

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:04.710
and then the other was
going to be the users.

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:06.835
So I want to sort of talk
about those in two steps.

00:06:06.835 --> 00:06:09.979
Talk about the team first,
and then the user second.

00:06:09.979 --> 00:06:12.270
So the first thing we knew
was going to be super, super

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:14.960
critical for our data team
in particular to accomplish

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:16.940
its goals was going
to be finding a way

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:20.000
to minimize the friction it took
to get new data in particular

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:21.020
in the warehouse.

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:23.061
This was something that
was a huge problem for us

00:06:23.061 --> 00:06:25.760
as our company went from
being a company that

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:27.532
made list posts that
lived on a website,

00:06:27.532 --> 00:06:29.490
to suddenly being a
company that was publishing

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:31.031
across a bunch of
different platforms

00:06:31.031 --> 00:06:33.530
and had content living
in all different places.

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:36.620
Every time we needed
to upload new data

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:40.370
before we had BigQuery, it was
this very, very laborious task

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:42.710
for us, where we were
writing new tables schemas,

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:45.106
writing migrations, bothering
people for backfill.

00:06:45.106 --> 00:06:46.980
And that was something
that just didn't work.

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:49.784
So when we set out into our
adventure of moving everything

00:06:49.784 --> 00:06:51.200
over into a BigQuery,
that was one

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:52.991
of the things that was
most important to us

00:06:52.991 --> 00:06:54.324
during the build out.

00:06:54.324 --> 00:06:56.990
So there were a couple of things
that we did in terms of the way

00:06:56.990 --> 00:07:00.010
that we architected
the system that

00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:02.040
have proven to be super,
super important for us

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:04.039
and allowed us to move
much more quickly than we

00:07:04.039 --> 00:07:05.149
were in the past.

00:07:05.149 --> 00:07:07.190
So the first one is that
all of our pipeline data

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:09.050
just goes straight into GCS.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:11.070
Seems like kind of a
simple face value thing,

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:12.110
but it's actually
super important,

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:13.526
because what that
means is that we

00:07:13.526 --> 00:07:15.920
don't have to do any
work as a data team

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:18.610
to get stuff into the place that
it needs to be for us to get it

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:19.550
into BigQuery.

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:22.860
If it passes through the
pipeline, it's there.

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:24.560
The second one was
that all it required

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:27.959
for us to actually import
data is writing a JSON schema.

00:07:27.959 --> 00:07:30.500
So previously, like I said, we
had to write these migrations,

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:32.240
we had to bother
people for backfill.

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:33.470
Now, all of that's built in.

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:35.844
All you do is write a config
for what you want your table

00:07:35.844 --> 00:07:36.470
to look like.

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:39.080
You deploy that,
your data is there.

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:41.360
You can deploy those tables
at any time with a review

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:42.151
from your teammate.

00:07:42.151 --> 00:07:44.540
You're not blocked by the
data engineering team.

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:46.047
And you get your
backfill for free,

00:07:46.047 --> 00:07:48.380
which is something that was
super, super crucial for us.

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:49.640
All you do is write
in a start date.

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:50.690
As long as the log
files are there,

00:07:50.690 --> 00:07:53.065
you'll get that data back to
whatever start date you set.

00:07:53.065 --> 00:07:55.370
So this has been huge for
us, and it's totally changed

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:57.234
the way that we think
about our workflow

00:07:57.234 --> 00:07:59.650
and actually getting the data
that we need into our system

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:01.582
so that we can surface
it to our end users.

00:08:01.582 --> 00:08:04.040
So once we've done that, we've
got a couple of other things

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:05.400
that were super important.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:07.400
And the biggest one
of these was actually

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:09.020
giving analysts
and data scientists

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:11.600
the tools they need to build
for less technical users.

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:13.580
So we had taken a
philosophy in the past

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:16.010
where we had actually built
a lot of stuff in house.

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:18.960
And when we were
just a publisher that

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:20.865
made list content that
lived on the website,

00:08:20.865 --> 00:08:23.240
like I said earlier, that
actually served us really well,

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:26.270
because we had something that
was custom built to our needs.

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:27.750
But it didn't scale very well.

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:30.590
As soon as we started publishing
on all these other platforms,

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:32.840
it became a really,
really big burden for us

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:35.690
to find a way to incorporate
that new stuff into dashboards,

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:38.150
because we were suddenly
relying on our engineering team

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:41.289
to help us do the whole process
of how do we redesign this,

00:08:41.289 --> 00:08:42.539
how do we do this whole thing.

00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:45.050
So we knew we needed a better
system that would allow

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:47.060
us to iterate more quickly.

00:08:47.060 --> 00:08:49.220
Like I said, this is sort
of the core of the way we

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:50.570
think about everything we do.

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:52.569
We really want to be able
to power that feedback

00:08:52.569 --> 00:08:54.290
loop for our content creators.

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:55.790
So what we ultimately
landed on was

00:08:55.790 --> 00:08:58.160
that we needed to use
a third party solution,

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:00.330
and the one that we
picked was Looker.

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:02.517
I'm sure some of you guys
are familiar with Looker,

00:09:02.517 --> 00:09:04.100
and I'm talking talk
a little bit more

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:07.530
about why it works for us,
and why it might work for you.

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:09.440
So why did we choose Looker?

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:11.840
Like I said, one of the
biggest things that we found

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:14.450
was going to be huge for us
was the minimal dev overhead.

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:15.950
You do need to have a
little bit of maintenance

00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:16.940
at the initial setup.

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:18.814
But once that's
sort of in place,

00:09:18.814 --> 00:09:21.230
your data team can actually
do a lot of the heavy lifting,

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:22.970
because Looker manages
a lot of the front end.

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:25.070
And you're really just sort
of managing the data modeling

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:26.736
piece, which is
something that most data

00:09:26.736 --> 00:09:28.545
teams can do on their own.

00:09:28.545 --> 00:09:30.920
The second thing that worked
really, really well for us--

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:32.810
and keep in mind those
sort of varying skill sets

00:09:32.810 --> 00:09:35.143
that I mentioned earlier when
I was talking about this--

00:09:35.143 --> 00:09:37.820
is that it can support both a
general and specific use case.

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:39.410
So what does that actually mean?

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:41.600
Looker actually has
created different versions

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:43.280
of their experience
for people who

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:45.990
have different levels of
comfort working with data.

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:48.175
So they call this explorer
versus dashboards.

00:09:48.175 --> 00:09:50.300
There are sort of different
ways that you can think

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:51.650
about what that actually means.

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:54.120
This example that you're
seeing here is a dashboard.

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:55.694
So this is a very
curated experience

00:09:55.694 --> 00:09:57.860
that someone who's a little
less comfortable working

00:09:57.860 --> 00:09:59.212
with raw data might see.

00:09:59.212 --> 00:10:01.170
This is actually a real
dashboard that we have.

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:03.980
This is showing you
completions and results

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:05.111
for a specific quiz.

00:10:05.111 --> 00:10:06.860
So if you're someone
who's writing quizzes

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:08.480
for BuzzFeed.com,
you want to come in,

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:09.740
you want to see what
results people are

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:12.320
getting, what they're choosing
for different questions.

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:14.540
We actually are able to
serve this all right to you

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:16.430
in a very curated Looker
experience where all

00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:18.120
you have to do is put in a URL.

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:20.459
So we're taking a lot of the
burden off of the end user

00:10:20.459 --> 00:10:23.000
in terms of having to understand
how that data is structured,

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:25.850
where it comes from, how to
pull it, how to manipulate it.

00:10:25.850 --> 00:10:28.100
They get the answers that
they need super quickly.

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:31.640
But we can also support a
much more specific use case

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:33.680
for someone like an
analyst, maybe someone

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:35.210
on our finance team.

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:36.982
So Looker calls this explore.

00:10:36.982 --> 00:10:38.690
The way that it looks
is it's essentially

00:10:38.690 --> 00:10:40.220
a big pivot table
that sits right

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:42.120
on top of your data warehouse.

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:45.110
You have someone on your
more technical data team

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:47.149
that comes in and sets
up the modeling layer,

00:10:47.149 --> 00:10:49.190
which will define the
dimensions and the measures

00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:51.020
and how those things
are aggregated.

00:10:51.020 --> 00:10:52.360
It's all SQL based.

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:54.380
It's a language
called LookML that's

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:57.464
kind of a weird hybrid
between YAML and SQL.

00:10:57.464 --> 00:10:58.880
But it's pretty
easy to work with,

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:02.545
and it allows us to support
these more specific use cases.

00:11:02.545 --> 00:11:04.670
So let's say our finance
team wants to come in here

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:06.920
and cut stuff up by months
and countries and all sorts

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:09.110
of different things that
most users don't need.

00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:11.670
They have access to do that
stuff right here in the tool.

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:13.440
And this is actually
the explorer

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:15.749
that undergirds this dashboard
I just showed you guys.

00:11:15.749 --> 00:11:17.790
So you can see how the
two things sort of connect

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:19.242
to each other.

00:11:19.242 --> 00:11:20.700
There were a couple
of other things

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:22.533
that we found worked
really well for us when

00:11:22.533 --> 00:11:25.489
we were looking at Looker
and a bunch of other tools.

00:11:25.489 --> 00:11:27.030
One of the other
key ones was that it

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:29.710
has tons of off-the-shelf
access controls.

00:11:29.710 --> 00:11:32.620
So this was important for
us for a couple of reasons.

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:35.402
First of all, we
have users that are

00:11:35.402 --> 00:11:37.110
working across these
different platforms.

00:11:37.110 --> 00:11:39.780
Some people might work on,
say, Facebook and YouTube.

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:41.196
But you might have
some people who

00:11:41.196 --> 00:11:42.927
work on just Snapchat,
or just Instagram.

00:11:42.927 --> 00:11:44.760
And one of the things
that we found early on

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:47.220
is that it was very confusing
and overwhelming for them

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:49.290
to be able to see just
everything if that's not

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:50.762
what they needed.

00:11:50.762 --> 00:11:53.220
So this allowed us to sort of
partition off the experience,

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:55.164
and make it a little
bit more digestible

00:11:55.164 --> 00:11:57.580
by only showing people the
things that they wanted to see,

00:11:57.580 --> 00:11:58.671
or they needed to see.

00:11:58.671 --> 00:12:00.420
Similarly, you could
imagine that's become

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:02.100
very useful if you
have sensitive data

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:03.052
in your instance.

00:12:03.052 --> 00:12:05.010
So the ways that a lot
of other people use this

00:12:05.010 --> 00:12:07.620
is that if they have, say,
revenue data or finance

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:09.624
data that they only want
specific teams to be

00:12:09.624 --> 00:12:11.790
able to see, you can kind
of use it in the same way.

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:13.290
We've done that a
little bit, but it's

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:15.330
something that's sort of
less important for the way

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:17.010
that we think about
how this data surfaced.

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:19.140
We really like to democratize
and make most things

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:20.650
available to most people.

00:12:20.650 --> 00:12:22.650
Another thing that's great
is that it's actually

00:12:22.650 --> 00:12:25.470
version controlled, so it's
connected directly to Git.

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:27.557
This is super helpful
for the modeling layer.

00:12:27.557 --> 00:12:29.640
It's also extremely helpful
when something breaks,

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:31.610
and you're trying to
figure out what happened.

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:34.920
You can actually chase it
back to a specific commit.

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:38.820
This has also been really
helpful because we're

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:41.040
relying pretty heavily
on a functionality

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:43.110
that Looker has
it's called PDTs--

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:44.832
Persistent Derived Tables.

00:12:44.832 --> 00:12:46.290
So what it allows
you to do is kind

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:48.420
of create your own
on-the-fly aggregations that

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:50.522
run on schedule and are cached.

00:12:50.522 --> 00:12:52.230
So if you, say, have
hourly data that you

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:54.240
want to roll up
daily or monthly,

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:56.014
you can actually do
this right in Looker.

00:12:56.014 --> 00:12:57.180
It's all version controlled.

00:12:57.180 --> 00:12:59.096
You can sort of see how
those aggregations are

00:12:59.096 --> 00:13:00.350
working, how they're changing.

00:13:00.350 --> 00:13:02.550
It's something that's worked
really well for us-- so much so

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:04.060
that we're actually
starting to steal it

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:06.185
into one of our own features,
which I'll talk about

00:13:06.185 --> 00:13:07.326
in a little bit.

00:13:07.326 --> 00:13:09.450
And then it's also been a
great prototyping ground.

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:12.990
So we still have
our homebuilt tools.

00:13:12.990 --> 00:13:14.872
We still rely on those
a bunch, sort of as

00:13:14.872 --> 00:13:16.830
the front page for the
way that a lot of people

00:13:16.830 --> 00:13:20.250
experience data, with
Looker being the deeper use

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:21.250
case that they have.

00:13:21.250 --> 00:13:22.791
So this has been a
great place for us

00:13:22.791 --> 00:13:26.132
to sort of test out
new data experiences

00:13:26.132 --> 00:13:27.840
that we want to make
available to people,

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:30.912
and potentially integrate
into our existing tools.

00:13:30.912 --> 00:13:32.370
Which is actually
something you can

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:34.830
see we've done with that same
quiz data that I showed you.

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:37.467
So now we have this
tool called Dashbird.

00:13:37.467 --> 00:13:39.300
This was the old dashboard
that I showed you

00:13:39.300 --> 00:13:40.830
about that was
created originally

00:13:40.830 --> 00:13:42.645
to show post performance.

00:13:42.645 --> 00:13:45.270
So we've now gone and taken that
more granular quiz performance

00:13:45.270 --> 00:13:46.980
data and just
incorporated it directly

00:13:46.980 --> 00:13:49.797
into the Dashbird experience
for all of our quizzes.

00:13:49.797 --> 00:13:51.630
So this is something
that users really like.

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:53.040
It reduces the friction.

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:55.980
They can kind of see everything
they want in one place.

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:58.650
And getting data like that
into this experience previously

00:13:58.650 --> 00:14:00.150
would have been
something that would

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:02.191
have required a lot more
work, a lot more effort,

00:14:02.191 --> 00:14:03.520
a lot more dev hours.

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:05.160
So it's a way that
we've sort of been

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:07.440
able to reduce the lead time
on some of these things,

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:09.660
and also test much more quickly.

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:11.160
I think we would
have been much more

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:13.612
hesitant to build
something like that,

00:14:13.612 --> 00:14:15.570
knowing that it would
have required so much dev

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:16.260
work previously.

00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:20.350
And now we were able to kind
of just plop it right in there.

00:14:20.350 --> 00:14:22.090
So I've talked a lot
about our data team.

00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:24.150
And I want to talk a
little bit about the way

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:26.525
that we think about building
for our non-technical users,

00:14:26.525 --> 00:14:29.820
because these are really the
people that are most important,

00:14:29.820 --> 00:14:31.800
and they're on the front lines.

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:33.714
So this boils down to
sort of four things

00:14:33.714 --> 00:14:35.130
that we've found
in our experience

00:14:35.130 --> 00:14:38.880
in the many iterations of
building within Looker.

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:41.340
The first one-- and this is
actually a huge reason that we

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:42.720
chose BigQuery--

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:44.889
is that people want
answers really quickly.

00:14:44.889 --> 00:14:47.430
We sort of were the architects
of our own demise in the sense

00:14:47.430 --> 00:14:49.346
that we had been very
thoughtful about the way

00:14:49.346 --> 00:14:51.870
that we had engineered a
lot of our homebuilt tools.

00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:55.354
So people were used to things
loading really, really quickly.

00:14:55.354 --> 00:14:57.270
So when we suddenly put
them in an environment

00:14:57.270 --> 00:15:01.350
where they were hitting a data
warehouse rather than, say,

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:03.685
[INAUDIBLE] or Redis cache.

00:15:03.685 --> 00:15:05.810
They were like, why is this
taking so long to load?

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:07.920
I'm used to, like, two
seconds, and suddenly I'm

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:09.410
waiting two minutes for things.

00:15:09.410 --> 00:15:11.400
So BigQuery was a way
for us to alleviate

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:13.337
a lot of those problems,
create an experience

00:15:13.337 --> 00:15:15.420
that was much faster and
much more attuned to what

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:16.875
people were used to.

00:15:16.875 --> 00:15:19.250
Particularly when you consider
that a lot of these people

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:21.001
are non-technical,
and their eyes kind of

00:15:21.001 --> 00:15:22.500
glaze over when you
start explaining

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:24.464
to them that the reason
that the thing is slow

00:15:24.464 --> 00:15:26.130
is not because they
did something wrong,

00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:27.630
or you did something
wrong, but just

00:15:27.630 --> 00:15:29.520
because the warehouse
is taking a long time,

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:32.380
or maybe it has a BigQuery
backlog right now.

00:15:32.380 --> 00:15:36.554
So that's a place where
BigQuery has helped us a lot.

00:15:36.554 --> 00:15:38.220
Another thing we've
found, and I sort of

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:40.440
alluded to this earlier,
is that less is more.

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:42.252
When we first started
working with Looker,

00:15:42.252 --> 00:15:43.710
we sort of were
like, oh my god, we

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:45.960
can suddenly open up the
whole warehouse to everybody.

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:47.190
This is so great.

00:15:47.190 --> 00:15:49.644
And that actually was
really bad because people

00:15:49.644 --> 00:15:51.060
got into this
experience, and they

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:53.820
were like, I don't know what
any of this stuff means.

00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:56.375
Why are there six
different view metrics?

00:15:56.375 --> 00:15:57.750
I don't understand
the difference

00:15:57.750 --> 00:15:59.070
between a time series.

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:02.070
Why does this number not
change at all when I add dates?

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:05.190
So we've really reeled back
a lot of that stuff, tried

00:16:05.190 --> 00:16:06.744
to simplify.

00:16:06.744 --> 00:16:08.910
Really tried to show people
sort of the bare minimum

00:16:08.910 --> 00:16:10.630
of what they need
as a starting point,

00:16:10.630 --> 00:16:13.510
and then scale up from there,
rather than starting off

00:16:13.510 --> 00:16:16.650
by opening up the floodgates.

00:16:16.650 --> 00:16:18.970
To that same point, clearly
labeled and described

00:16:18.970 --> 00:16:20.890
fields are everything.

00:16:20.890 --> 00:16:23.500
We made a huge mistake
early on of relying

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:25.541
on less technical
users to understand,

00:16:25.541 --> 00:16:27.665
oh, this is an aggregate,
this is not an aggregate.

00:16:27.665 --> 00:16:31.390
It just was a big mistake, and
we actually lost a lot of trust

00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:34.060
that it took us a little
while to build back.

00:16:34.060 --> 00:16:36.640
So this is something that I
can't emphasize highly enough.

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:39.010
And as we've worked through
a lot of these challenges,

00:16:39.010 --> 00:16:41.560
we've actually impose
a very strict style

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:43.316
guide on our
technical users that

00:16:43.316 --> 00:16:45.190
is requiring them to do
a lot of these things

00:16:45.190 --> 00:16:47.600
because of how important
we found it to be.

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:49.600
And then the last
piece, in tune to what

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:51.825
I showed you guys
on the last slide.

00:16:51.825 --> 00:16:53.950
We've really tried not to
fight existing work flows

00:16:53.950 --> 00:16:54.920
if we don't have to.

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:57.670
So if users already have a tool
that they're used to going to,

00:16:57.670 --> 00:16:59.080
if there's a way for
you to sort of integrate

00:16:59.080 --> 00:17:00.871
the different things
you're doing together,

00:17:00.871 --> 00:17:04.410
that's something that we've
found works super, super well.

00:17:04.410 --> 00:17:07.260
So now that I've walked you
guys through all of this,

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:08.700
where has it actually gotten us?

00:17:08.700 --> 00:17:10.599
Where are we today?

00:17:10.599 --> 00:17:14.530
So this is our usage as
of a couple of weeks ago.

00:17:14.530 --> 00:17:16.410
So we have hundreds
of terabytes of data

00:17:16.410 --> 00:17:19.530
across 300-plus tables
accessible to our analysts

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:21.000
and our users via BigQuery.

00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:22.666
I've been told that
we actually are just

00:17:22.666 --> 00:17:25.670
starting to cross into
petabytes territory now,

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:28.349
with some of the new stuff
that we've been importing.

00:17:28.349 --> 00:17:31.080
We're running over
100,000 queries a day.

00:17:31.080 --> 00:17:33.145
So bear in mind I've
talked a lot about Looker

00:17:33.145 --> 00:17:34.770
and supporting our
non-technical users,

00:17:34.770 --> 00:17:36.659
but BigQuery is
also supporting all

00:17:36.659 --> 00:17:38.200
of our different
technical use cases.

00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:42.150
So our machine learning models,
all of the sort of regularly

00:17:42.150 --> 00:17:43.020
scheduled jobs.

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:45.270
So you can see
Looker is actually

00:17:45.270 --> 00:17:48.510
is only about 5% to 10% of
our total workflow in a day,

00:17:48.510 --> 00:17:51.120
but we're really relying
on BigQuery super

00:17:51.120 --> 00:17:54.099
heavily for all
of our data needs.

00:17:54.099 --> 00:17:56.640
And then one of the things that
we're actually super proud of

00:17:56.640 --> 00:18:00.060
is that we have more than half
of our 1,500 employees using

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:03.180
Looker each month, which I think
really speaks to what we've

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:05.760
been able to accomplish here,
especially when you consider

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:07.770
the fact that most
of those users

00:18:07.770 --> 00:18:11.120
are people that are not used
to interacting with a BI tool,

00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:14.200
and are usually content
creators, writers, editors,

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:16.560
that sort of thing.

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:19.230
So a little bit in terms
of what's next for us.

00:18:22.050 --> 00:18:24.237
We're starting to
experiment with a new tool

00:18:24.237 --> 00:18:26.820
that you guys are going to hear
more about today and tomorrow.

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:29.640
We were one of the early access
partners for BigQuery Machine

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:30.650
Learning.

00:18:30.650 --> 00:18:34.080
This is something that we're
really excited about because it

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:37.410
allows us to really
open up machine learning

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:40.710
to a lot more of our analysts,
whereas previously there

00:18:40.710 --> 00:18:43.590
were much more stringent
technical requirements

00:18:43.590 --> 00:18:46.900
in terms of who can
do that type of work.

00:18:46.900 --> 00:18:50.040
This is also something that's
been developed in partnership

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:51.810
with Looker, which is
really cool for us,

00:18:51.810 --> 00:18:53.700
because it allows us
to sort of just plug

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:58.950
in and see some things, like our
model performance statistics,

00:18:58.950 --> 00:19:02.010
right in the Looker experience
that we're used to using.

00:19:02.010 --> 00:19:03.859
To that end, we're
actually continuing

00:19:03.859 --> 00:19:06.150
to expand pretty heavily on
our existing recommendation

00:19:06.150 --> 00:19:06.990
systems.

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:08.970
So this is something
that actually

00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:11.880
powers a lot of what you
see on the BuzzFeed site,

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:13.979
and even what you see
on our Facebook pages.

00:19:13.979 --> 00:19:15.520
It's all happening
behind the scenes,

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:17.710
but it's stuff that you
might not realize is there.

00:19:17.710 --> 00:19:20.455
So it's the way that
we optimize our feeds.

00:19:20.455 --> 00:19:22.080
This is the way that
we're recommending

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:23.700
content to our
social media editors

00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:25.680
to post on our different
Facebook pages.

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:27.240
We have about 140
of those, so you

00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:30.000
can imagine that that gets to
be a pretty complicated problem

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:30.696
for them.

00:19:30.696 --> 00:19:32.820
So this is the place where
we're investing heavily.

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:36.630
And our use of GCP
and BigQuery has

00:19:36.630 --> 00:19:39.030
allowed us to really reduce
a lot of the friction

00:19:39.030 --> 00:19:41.887
there and move
much more quickly.

00:19:41.887 --> 00:19:44.220
And then we're also working
on our own materialized view

00:19:44.220 --> 00:19:45.460
solution.

00:19:45.460 --> 00:19:49.680
So we actually just finished
our version 1 launch of this,

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:51.690
and are starting to
launch on version 2.

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:55.230
So this is the solution
that's akin to Looker's

00:19:55.230 --> 00:19:57.460
persistent derived
table solution.

00:19:57.460 --> 00:19:59.520
So we really liked
the idea of being

00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:03.210
able to have cached aggregations
that we could access,

00:20:03.210 --> 00:20:06.390
but what we didn't like was
having that locked specifically

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:08.100
into Looker, because
we were running

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:11.880
into these scenarios where, say,
a data scientist was working

00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:16.710
on a model, and wanted to
access our denormalized Facebook

00:20:16.710 --> 00:20:17.464
statistics.

00:20:17.464 --> 00:20:18.630
And that was only in Looker.

00:20:18.630 --> 00:20:20.670
And suddenly you're
hitting Looker API,

00:20:20.670 --> 00:20:23.170
and then having to do all
this additional manipulation.

00:20:23.170 --> 00:20:27.240
So what we decided to do was
actually build our own solution

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:27.850
for this.

00:20:27.850 --> 00:20:29.680
And our data engineering
team-- again,

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:31.170
is sitting right
there-- has been

00:20:31.170 --> 00:20:33.600
so great through this process.

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:36.330
And I personally, as
a big user of this,

00:20:36.330 --> 00:20:39.240
could not be more thrilled with
the way that it's turned out.

00:20:39.240 --> 00:20:40.740
But this is something
that we think

00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:42.240
is going to be
really crucial for us

00:20:42.240 --> 00:20:45.690
in terms of upping our game in
terms of our data governance,

00:20:45.690 --> 00:20:49.470
having really consistent ways
that we think about cleaning up

00:20:49.470 --> 00:20:51.340
our data, aggregating our data.

00:20:51.340 --> 00:20:53.070
So this is actually
a bit of the work

00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:55.770
that I'm sort of most
excited about right now.

00:20:55.770 --> 00:20:59.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

